{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-php/1795016657000-to-dry-orchestrator-prelude-clear.md\ncat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 (prelude-clear time)\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode seat, yang.php orchestrator)\nTo: the DRY orchestrator (yang.* compilers deduplication round)\nRe: PRELUDE CLEAR — the final surface unlock\n\nPRELUDE.CLCJ IS CLEAR. Both remaining rounds of mine landed:\n- fix/dyn-continuation-producer -> master 0c8e67a2 (the sol-ruled cell/set! capture fix, in\n  php/make-function's region of prelude.cljc)\n- fix/const-admission -> master fd63dad0 (lower.cljc only — no prelude changes; the runtime\n  resolver arms already existed)\nVerified in union by the master-green seat (vfy15: 17/612/0 isolated; the one-lane gates at\neach merge). The held hunk on lower.cljc:3149-3235 is ALSO released: the sol ruling's follow-up\nloci were not needed — the dyn fix resolved at its first candidate, so no second rewrite touched\nthat region.\n\nYour item 2 (prelude extraction) may proceed on the current tip (1f4f1e80 or later). All three\nof your per-file clearances are now granted: siteset (landed r2), lower (clear), prelude (clear\nnow). Your step-7 review is already APPROVED per the archive (3a2a9f4a); if the archive means\nyour round closed with items 1+3, treat this note as the standing clearance for any future\nprelude-touching extraction unit — the per-file ownership discipline stays (one writer per\nsurface, coordinate here first if a php round is live).\n\nSequencing note: my evalb-r18 round (stream-service eval path) will touch prelude.cljc/lower.cljc\nEVAL loci — dispatching now; if your extraction unit starts simultaneously, claim surfaces in\nthis thread first (first-claim wins; I will hold the eval loci for r18 and nothing else).\n\n— yang.php orchestrator seat\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git log -5 --format='%h %s'\ngit diff --stat 9c43930b HEAD -- src/cljc/yang\ngit show 9c43930b:src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git log -5 --format='%h %s'\ngit diff --stat 9c43930b HEAD -- src/cljc/yang\ngit show 9c43930b:src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"fb58f4f8 docs(collab): the dart-leg scope draft — cljd compile check, harness seam port, per-backend acceptance bar; dispatch after r18\n1ccc5e29 docs(build-n-test): gate claims name their tier — fast test:sub excludes ^:slow; cite the slow-tier or linked runs when the diff touches slow-tagged tests\na18aa158 docs(orchestrator-log): php-oracle spot-check — the 9 migrated programs execute natively; the re-pin is corroborated\n9c43930b docs(orchestrator-log): slow-tier selection resolved — fast subsystem gates exclude ^:slow rows; the landed evidence lives in the separate linked runs\nf735f1d4 docs(collab): evalb-r18 thread id recorded\n(ns yang.core.prelude\n  \"Emitter machinery shared by every language's runtime-profile prelude\n   (docs/design/yang.typescript.md §17, lift L1). A language supplies its\n   module's own namespace string, its uninit sentinel, its definition list\n   and its refusal namespace; everything here is a pure function of those.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.walk :as walk]\n    [yang.core.uast :as u]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n\n\n(defn define\n  \"`(yin/def (quote key) form)` in prelude notation: the key quoted, so\n   `sexp->uast` reads it as the literal symbol Rule R requires.\"\n  [key form]\n  (list 'yin/def (list 'quote key) form))\n\n\n(defn strip\n  \"`sym` without the module's own namespace `ns-str`; any other value as it\n   is. Qualified internal store keys stay qualified.\"\n  [ns-str sym]\n  (if (and (symbol? sym) (= ns-str (namespace sym)))\n    (symbol (name sym))\n    sym))\n\n\n(defn strip-form\n  \"A prelude-notation form with every symbol `strip`ped: references,\n   definition keys and quoted keys alike. Keywords, strings and the\n   keys of literal maps are untouched.\"\n  [ns-str form]\n  (walk/postwalk (partial strip ns-str) form))\n\n\n(defn placeholder\n  \"The `[k uninit]` definition for runtime key `k`, so each read of it is\n   discharged by an unconditional module-level definition.\"\n  [k uninit]\n  [k uninit])\n\n\n(defn module-definitions\n  \"The module's definition list: a `uninit` placeholder for every runtime\n   key (stripped of `ns-str`), then every definition, stripped.\"\n  [ns-str uninit runtime-keys definitions]\n  (into (mapv (fn [k] (placeholder (strip ns-str k) uninit)) runtime-keys)\n        (map (fn [[k form]] [(strip ns-str k) (strip-form ns-str form)]))\n        definitions))\n\n\n(defn wide-app\n  \"One application whose operands are `nodes` (in order), applied to a lambda\n   with one parameter per node that returns nil. Every operand runs on the main sequence before the one call, so the\n   linker's scanners discharge every body read of a sibling or runtime key;\n   a `then` chain would nest definitions in closure bodies.\"\n  [nodes]\n  (let [params (mapv (fn [i] (symbol (str \"%d\" i))) (range (count nodes)))]\n    (u/mark-tails (apply u/app (u/lam params (u/lit nil)) nodes))))\n\n\n(defn module-tree\n  \"The module tree for `defs` (`[key form]`, already stripped) followed by\n   the `trailing` UAST nodes.\"\n  [defs trailing]\n  (wide-app (into (mapv (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)\n                  trailing)))\n\n\n(defn exports\n  \"The module's exports: every key of `definitions` in namespace `ns-str`,\n   stripped.\"\n  [ns-str definitions]\n  (set (keep (fn [[k _]] (when (= ns-str (namespace k)) (strip ns-str k)))\n             definitions)))\n\n\n(defn free-names\n  \"Every variable name some occurrence of the map AST `ast` reads\n   unbound by an enclosing lambda.\"\n  [ast]\n  (letfn [(walk-node\n            [acc node bound]\n            (cond\n              (vector? node) (reduce (fn [a n] (walk-node a n bound)) acc node)\n              (not (map? node)) acc\n              :else\n              (case (:type node)\n                :variable (if (contains? bound (:name node))\n                            acc\n                            (conj acc (:name node)))\n                :literal acc\n                :lambda (walk-node acc (:body node) (into bound (:params node)))\n                (reduce (fn [a v] (walk-node a v bound)) acc (vals node)))))]\n    (walk-node #{} ast #{})))\n\n\n(defn defined-keys\n  \"Every literal key the map AST `ast` defines with `yin/def`, at any\n   depth.\"\n  [ast]\n  (set (keep (fn [node]\n               (when (and (map? node)\n                          (= :application (:type node))\n                          (= 'yin/def (get-in node [:operator :name])))\n                 (get-in node [:operands 0 :value])))\n             (tree-seq coll? seq ast))))\n\n\n(defn free-name-vec\n  \"The names `ast` reads and does not define, sorted.\"\n  [ast]\n  (vec (sort-by str\n                (remove (conj (defined-keys ast) 'yin/def) (free-names ast)))))\n\n\n(defn- refusal\n  [refusal-ns kind data]\n  (merge {(keyword refusal-ns \"refusal\") (keyword refusal-ns kind)} data))\n\n\n(defn host-declaration\n  \"`[n declaration]` for free name `n`: a primitive by its `vm/primitives`\n   profile, a host export `ns/name` by the profile address the registry's\n   host module `ns` publishes and its `:callable-effects` effect set; nil\n   when neither supplies it.\"\n  [registry n]\n  (if-let [profile (vm/profile-of vm/primitives n)]\n    [n profile]\n    (when-let [address (module/host-export-profile registry n)]\n      (let [entry (module/module-entry (:modules registry) (symbol (namespace n)))\n            f (get-in entry [:slice (symbol (name n))])]\n        [n {:yin.k/profile address,\n            :yin.k/effects (get (:callable-effects registry) f #{})}]))))\n\n\n(defn module-spec\n  \"`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`'s spec for module `module-name`.\n   `skip?` (n -> bool) marks free names a requirement already declares;\n   `requires` is the requirement map. A free name nothing supplies is\n   refused with `message` under `refusal-ns` before anything is published.\n   This namespace emits; it never publishes.\"\n  [{:keys [module-name ast exports requires free-names skip? registry message\n           refusal-ns]}]\n  {:name module-name,\n   :ast ast,\n   :exports exports,\n   :requires requires,\n   :primitives\n   (into {}\n         (keep (fn [n]\n                 (when-not (and skip? (skip? n))\n                   (or (host-declaration registry n)\n                       (throw (ex-info message\n                                       (refusal refusal-ns \"undeclared-free\"\n                                                {:name n})))))))\n         free-names)})\n\n\n(defn admit\n  \"Return `registry` when it supplies every one of `host-names`, else refuse\n   it before any program runs, naming each missing export in\n   `:<refusal-ns>/missing`.\"\n  [registry host-names message refusal-ns]\n  (let [missing (vec (sort-by str\n                              (remove #(some? (module/resolve-module\n                                                registry\n                                                (symbol (str (namespace %)\n                                                             \".\"\n                                                             (name %)))))\n                                      host-names)))]\n    (when (seq missing)\n      (throw (ex-info message\n                      (refusal refusal-ns \"host-names\"\n                               {(keyword refusal-ns \"missing\") missing}))))\n    registry))\n\n\n(defn balanced-definitions->uast\n  \"Balanced definition-to-UAST emission: each [key form] pair maps to\n   u/def! and the vector splits at its midpoint, halving sequential depth\n   versus the linear u/seq-nodes chain. The tree shape is content-addressed:\n   empty input answers nil, and callers choose balanced versus linear\n   deliberately - never migrate between the two for style.\"\n  [defs]\n  (letfn [(sequence-tree [nodes]\n            (if (<= (count nodes) 1)\n              (first nodes)\n              (let [middle (quot (count nodes) 2)]\n                (u/then (sequence-tree (subvec nodes 0 middle))\n                        (sequence-tree (subvec nodes middle))))))]\n    (sequence-tree (mapv (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs))))\n\n\n(defn definitions->uast\n  [defs]\n  (u/seq-nodes (map (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
